WEBVTT
NOTE how-does-photolithography-work - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.453
Air or glass would swallow the beam that
prints a modern chip's smallest features.

00:00:04.453 --> 00:00:09.259
Photolithography copies a circuit pattern
onto a silicon wafer by shining light

00:00:09.259 --> 00:00:13.030
through a patterned mask onto a
light-sensitive coating called

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:17.045
photoresist, which changes its chemistry
wherever the light lands.

00:00:17.045 --> 00:00:22.638
In the most advanced machines that light
has a wavelength of 13.5 nanometres, as

00:00:22.638 --> 00:00:26.832
the engineering literature published
through IEEE describes.

00:00:26.832 --> 00:00:28.304
People quote that figure.

00:00:28.304 --> 00:00:33.193
It sets a scale, and it explains a great
deal about why these machines look the way

00:00:33.193 --> 00:00:33.664
they do.

00:00:33.664 --> 00:00:38.496
It does not explain how factories have
spent decades printing lines far narrower

00:00:38.496 --> 00:00:42.000
than the light that drew them. That gap is
the real story.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.503
Start with a polished silicon wafer.

00:00:44.503 --> 00:00:49.163
The first step is to coat it with a thin,
even film of photoresist.

00:00:49.163 --> 00:00:54.355
Usually liquid is dripped onto the wafer
and the wafer is spun so the film spreads

00:00:54.355 --> 00:00:54.672
flat.

00:00:54.672 --> 00:00:56.620
A gentle bake drives off solvent.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:01.461
Before each exposure, the machine finds
alignment marks left by earlier layers, so

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:04.000
the new pattern sits on top of the old
one.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.468
Then you expose the wafer.

00:01:05.468 --> 00:01:09.702
Light passes through a mask, which is a
plate of glass carrying the circuit

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:11.227
pattern in an opaque layer.

00:01:11.227 --> 00:01:16.405
An optical system projects a reduced image
of that pattern onto the resist.

00:01:16.405 --> 00:01:21.191
Next you develop it, washing the wafer in
a solution that dissolves either the

00:01:21.191 --> 00:01:23.216
exposed or the unexposed regions.

00:01:23.216 --> 00:01:24.784
What remains is a stencil.

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:29.366
Finally you etch, implant or deposit
material through the openings, and then

00:01:29.366 --> 00:01:30.933
you strip the resist away.

00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:34.513
That whole sequence is repeated layer
after layer.

00:01:34.513 --> 00:01:37.520
A finished chip is the accumulated result.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.397
Nothing in that list is exotic in
principle.

00:01:40.397 --> 00:01:45.170
University cleanrooms, including
facilities at MIT, teach the sequence to

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:49.878
students with simple contact printers,
where the mask touches the resist

00:01:49.878 --> 00:01:50.467
directly.

00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:55.016
The industrial version differs in scale
and tolerance. The idea is the same.

00:01:55.016 --> 00:02:00.171
In a factory the mask is projected rather
than pressed, and the lens shrinks the

00:02:00.171 --> 00:02:00.557
image.

00:02:00.557 --> 00:02:05.311
The wafer is stepped or scanned beneath
the optics so the same pattern lands in

00:02:05.311 --> 00:02:07.176
many places across its surface.

00:02:07.176 --> 00:02:12.751
The machine is enormous, but each exposure
is still a shadow falling on a chemical

00:02:12.751 --> 00:02:13.091
film.

00:02:13.091 --> 00:02:17.603
Photoresist is a coating whose solubility
changes where light lands.

00:02:17.603 --> 00:02:20.125
It comes in positive or negative tone.

00:02:20.125 --> 00:02:24.955
In a positive resist, exposed regions
become easier to dissolve, so the

00:02:24.955 --> 00:02:26.520
developer removes them.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:31.450
In a negative resist, exposure makes the
material harder to dissolve, so the

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.267
exposed regions stay behind.

00:02:33.267 --> 00:02:39.032
In both cases the resist converts an
optical image into a chemical one.

00:02:39.032 --> 00:02:42.653
The rest of the process responds to the
chemistry, not the light.

00:02:42.653 --> 00:02:46.937
Most modern resists add a trick called
chemical amplification.

00:02:46.937 --> 00:02:51.981
Inside the film sits a compound that
releases acid when it absorbs light.

00:02:51.981 --> 00:02:57.192
A photon is absorbed, an acid is released,
and then a bake spreads the reaction.

00:02:57.192 --> 00:03:02.212
That acid molecule wanders a short
distance and catalyses many chemical

00:03:02.212 --> 00:03:06.029
changes in the surrounding polymer before
it is spent.

00:03:06.029 --> 00:03:10.185
Where enough of those changes accumulate,
the pattern becomes soluble.

00:03:10.185 --> 00:03:14.697
Amplification means each exposure needs
fewer photons, which matters because

00:03:14.697 --> 00:03:17.309
short-wavelength light is expensive to
make.

00:03:17.309 --> 00:03:22.139
It also means the edge of every line
depends partly on where acid molecules

00:03:22.139 --> 00:03:23.299
happened to drift.

00:03:23.299 --> 00:03:28.112
That drift causes roughness at the edges
of lines, and researchers publishing

00:03:28.112 --> 00:03:30.237
through IEEE continue to study it.

00:03:30.237 --> 00:03:31.545
The footage is real.

00:03:31.545 --> 00:03:36.778
The documentary around it is constructed —
lights, music, editing, narration all

00:03:36.778 --> 00:03:38.413
telling you what to feel.

00:03:38.413 --> 00:03:41.716
Diffraction limits the sharpness of any
projected image.

00:03:41.716 --> 00:03:46.257
Light passing through a fine opening
spreads out, and a lens can only collect

00:03:46.257 --> 00:03:47.613
so much of that spread.

00:03:47.613 --> 00:03:51.461
Engineers often describe the relationship
with the Rayleigh criterion.

00:03:51.461 --> 00:03:55.968
The smallest printable feature scales with
the wavelength of the light, divided by

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:59.926
the numerical aperture of the lens,
multiplied by a factor that captures

00:03:59.926 --> 00:04:01.795
everything else about the process.

00:04:01.795 --> 00:04:04.730
A shorter wavelength gives smaller
features.

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:08.265
A wider collecting angle also gives
smaller features.

00:04:08.265 --> 00:04:10.933
A cleverer process shrinks them further.

00:04:10.933 --> 00:04:15.493
For that reason, the history of the
industry reads as a march down the

00:04:15.493 --> 00:04:18.865
The IEEE literature traces it in steps.

00:04:18.865 --> 00:04:23.275
Mercury lamps emitted the i-line at 365
nanometres.

00:04:23.275 --> 00:04:28.775
Krypton fluoride lasers, written KrF,
followed at 248 nanometres, and argon

00:04:28.775 --> 00:04:33.029
fluoride lasers, written ArF, came next at
193 nanometres.

00:04:33.029 --> 00:04:36.495
After that the industry paused for a long
time.

00:04:36.495 --> 00:04:42.541
Going shorter than 193 proved so difficult
that manufacturers squeezed more out of

00:04:42.541 --> 00:04:47.776
the same light for years before extreme
ultraviolet, or EUV, was ready.

00:04:47.776 --> 00:04:49.938
One way to do that was immersion.

00:04:49.938 --> 00:04:53.541
A thin layer of water is placed between
lens and wafer.

00:04:53.541 --> 00:04:58.152
Water bends light more strongly than air,
so the lens can accept a wider cone of

00:04:58.152 --> 00:05:01.323
light, which effectively raises the
numerical aperture.

00:05:01.323 --> 00:05:04.334
Immersion is not a new wavelength.

00:05:04.334 --> 00:05:11.596
IEEE papers on immersion describe it as
the same 193 nanometre light, focused more

00:05:11.596 --> 00:05:12.304
tightly.

00:05:12.304 --> 00:05:17.259
Immersion moves the second term of the
equation and leaves the first unchanged.

00:05:17.259 --> 00:05:22.701
Extreme ultraviolet light at 13.5
nanometres, the exposure wavelength

00:05:22.701 --> 00:05:27.749
documented across IEEE venues, is absorbed
by almost everything.

00:05:27.749 --> 00:05:31.764
That includes air and every glass that
could form a lens.

00:05:31.764 --> 00:05:34.933
So an EUV scanner works without glass
lenses.

00:05:34.933 --> 00:05:39.291
The whole light path runs in vacuum, and
curved mirrors form the image.

00:05:39.291 --> 00:05:42.969
Even the mask is a mirror instead of a
transparent plate.

00:05:42.969 --> 00:05:48.324
Each mirror is a stack of many alternating
ultrathin layers, classically molybdenum

00:05:48.324 --> 00:05:49.099
and silicon.

00:05:49.099 --> 00:05:54.011
The layers are tuned so that weak
reflections from every interface add

00:05:54.011 --> 00:05:56.601
No single surface reflects this light
well.

00:05:56.601 --> 00:06:01.480
The stack reflects it collectively, and it
still loses a substantial share of the

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:02.805
light at every bounce.

00:06:02.805 --> 00:06:08.055
NIST has worked on measuring how these
optics reflect and how they degrade under

00:06:08.055 --> 00:06:08.645
exposure.

00:06:08.645 --> 00:06:12.799
Making 13.5 nanometre light at all
requires a plasma.

00:06:12.799 --> 00:06:17.344
The IEEE literature describes how
production sources work.

00:06:17.344 --> 00:06:22.389
Small droplets of molten tin are struck by
a pulsed laser, which heats them into a

00:06:22.389 --> 00:06:25.157
plasma that emits in the extreme
ultraviolet.

00:06:25.157 --> 00:06:28.736
The machine then has to keep tin debris
off the collecting mirror.

00:06:28.736 --> 00:06:33.817
Only a small fraction of the light the
plasma produces ever reaches the wafer.

00:06:33.817 --> 00:06:37.531
That loss is one reason amplified resists
matter so much.

00:06:37.531 --> 00:06:43.455
Mirror systems have their own numerical
aperture, a dimensionless number.

00:06:43.455 --> 00:06:49.867
IEEE publications describe current EUV
tools with a numerical aperture of 0.33.

00:06:49.867 --> 00:06:55.640
Newer high numerical aperture designs
raise it to 0.55, at the cost of more

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:56.795
complex optics.

00:06:56.795 --> 00:07:00.246
Those two numbers show that the Rayleigh
logic still holds.

00:07:00.246 --> 00:07:05.042
When the wavelength cannot easily shrink
further, the collecting angle becomes the

00:07:05.042 --> 00:07:05.685
next lever.

00:07:05.685 --> 00:07:10.384
The answer to the cold open's puzzle lies
in the third term, the process factor.

00:07:10.384 --> 00:07:13.222
Wavelength and numerical aperture are
hardware.

00:07:13.222 --> 00:07:17.932
The process factor covers everything
engineers can do with the mask, the light

00:07:17.932 --> 00:07:19.019
and the chemistry.

00:07:19.019 --> 00:07:23.860
Engineers have pushed it down by refusing
to treat the mask as a literal picture of

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:24.560
the circuit.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:27.555
One technique is optical proximity
correction.

00:07:27.555 --> 00:07:32.178
Diffraction rounds corners and shortens
line ends, so the mask is drawn

00:07:32.178 --> 00:07:37.647
deliberately wrong, with small extra tabs
and bars, and the blurred image prints the

00:07:37.647 --> 00:07:38.624
intended shape.

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:40.877
Another technique is the phase-shift mask.

00:07:40.877 --> 00:07:45.275
It delays the light through some openings
relative to their neighbours, so the two

00:07:45.275 --> 00:07:49.029
waves cancel at the boundary between them
and the edge prints sharper.

00:07:49.029 --> 00:07:50.975
A third is multiple patterning.

00:07:50.975 --> 00:07:55.995
A pattern too dense for one exposure is
split across two or more masks, and each

00:07:55.995 --> 00:07:57.877
is printed and etched in turn.

00:07:57.877 --> 00:08:02.249
The combined result is finer than any
single exposure could reach.

00:08:02.249 --> 00:08:07.217
Nobody yet knows how far the process
factor can fall before physics or cost

00:08:07.217 --> 00:08:07.813
stops it.

00:08:07.813 --> 00:08:09.990
None of this breaks the diffraction limit.

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:14.345
These techniques work within the limit by
shaping the light so the blur lands in the

00:08:14.345 --> 00:08:15.019
right places.

00:08:15.019 --> 00:08:20.900
That is how factories printed lines far
narrower than 193 nanometres with the 193

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:23.877
nanometre light described in IEEE papers.

00:08:23.877 --> 00:08:28.868
It is also why the 13.5 nanometre figure,
impressive as it is, describes the

00:08:28.868 --> 00:08:32.480
starting point of the problem rather than
its solution.

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:36.560
Checking that the printed lines have the
right width is its own discipline.

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:41.560
Factories measure critical dimensions, the
widths of the most important features.

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:46.560
One tool is the scanning electron
microscope, which images the resist from

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:52.011
Another is scatterometry, which infers the
shape of a repeating pattern from how it

00:08:52.011 --> 00:08:53.061
diffracts light.

00:08:53.061 --> 00:08:57.635
Factories also measure overlay, which
tells them whether each layer landed on

00:08:57.635 --> 00:09:00.011
top of the previous one where it should.

00:09:00.011 --> 00:09:04.934
NIST works on reference measurements of
critical dimensions, so that a width

00:09:04.934 --> 00:09:08.368
measured in one tool means the same thing
in another.

00:09:08.368 --> 00:09:13.043
SEMI, the industry association, publishes
standards for wafers and masks.

00:09:13.043 --> 00:09:18.037
Those shared definitions let equipment
from different suppliers work together.

00:09:18.037 --> 00:09:23.268
The confirmation that photolithography
works at a given scale comes mostly after

00:09:23.268 --> 00:09:23.856
the fact.

00:09:23.856 --> 00:09:28.323
Engineers measure with the electron
microscope, with scatterometry and with

00:09:28.323 --> 00:09:29.216
overlay checks.

00:09:29.216 --> 00:09:32.960
They cut finished devices open and image
the cross-sections.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:37.578
The final test is whether the transistors
behave electrically as designed.

00:09:37.578 --> 00:09:41.509
The physics predicts the result, and the
measurements check it.

00:09:41.509 --> 00:09:46.665
Renderings of lithography usually show a
beam of glowing violet light falling onto

00:09:46.665 --> 00:09:47.168
a wafer.

00:09:47.168 --> 00:09:52.442
Extreme ultraviolet is invisible, and it
travels in vacuum inside a closed machine,

00:09:52.442 --> 00:09:55.237
so any glow you see was the artist's
choice.

00:09:55.237 --> 00:10:00.525
Animations often show a crisp, finished
circuit appearing in one flash.

00:10:00.525 --> 00:10:05.813
In reality, one exposure makes one hidden
image in one layer of resist.

00:10:05.813 --> 00:10:10.715
Nothing shows until development, and even
then you may need a microscope to see it.

00:10:10.715 --> 00:10:14.453
The yellow light in cleanroom footage, by
contrast, is real.

00:10:14.453 --> 00:10:19.188
That lighting filters out the short
wavelengths that would expose the resist

00:10:19.188 --> 00:10:19.936
by accident.

00:10:19.936 --> 00:10:24.825
The optical principle is settled physics,
and the wavelengths used are published and

00:10:24.825 --> 00:10:25.232
stable.

00:10:25.232 --> 00:10:30.635
Public sources do not give you the exact
process recipes inside any particular

00:10:30.635 --> 00:10:31.189
factory.

00:10:31.189 --> 00:10:36.107
Resist formulations, bake conditions, mask
corrections and dose settings are

00:10:36.107 --> 00:10:36.883
proprietary.

00:10:36.883 --> 00:10:42.254
Outside accounts can describe the method
in general, but they give specific numbers

00:10:42.254 --> 00:10:45.360
only where a manufacturer chooses to share
them.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:49.552
The marketing names for chip generations
sound like measurements, but they no

00:10:49.552 --> 00:10:53.472
longer correspond to any single physical
width, so treat them as labels.

00:10:53.472 --> 00:10:58.412
Claims about how far the process factor
can fall, or when a shorter wavelength

00:10:58.412 --> 00:11:01.072
might arrive, are forecasts, not findings.

00:11:01.072 --> 00:11:05.717
Every layer of a chip began as a pattern
of light on a chemical film, developed

00:11:05.717 --> 00:11:08.011
into a stencil and then made permanent.

00:11:08.011 --> 00:11:09.729
The wavelength sets the scale.

00:11:09.729 --> 00:11:14.140
The clever work happens in the gap between
that scale and the lines a factory

00:11:14.140 --> 00:11:15.056
actually prints.
